committee":"City Council","date":"11/05/2026","key":true}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-21","committee":"Homes, Economy and Infrastructure Scrutiny Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Executive Board","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Corporate Scrutiny Committee","time":"9.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Health Scrutiny Committee","time":"9.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-25","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"9.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-16","committee":"Nottinghamshire and City of Nottingham Fire and Rescue Authority - Finance and Resources","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-20","committee":"Executive Board","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-22","committee":"Health Scrutiny Committee","time":"9.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-01","committee":"Executive Board","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"People, Communities and Environment Scrutiny Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"City Council","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Companies Governance Executive Committee","time":"9.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Children and Adults Scrutiny Committee","time":"9.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"EMSS operational reserves fully depleted; partnership now has no financial buffer against overspends","lead":"Nottingham City's financial resilience is affected by its reliance on EMSS; the elimination of reserves means unexpected operational costs must now be funded immediately from the council's own budget rather than from partnership contingencies","iso":"2026-09-21","paper_title":"EMSS Finance Report JC Sep 26 (Final)","paper_url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk/documents/s185210/185210.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Nottingham Castle loan was removed from the 2026/27 Capital Strategy","lead":"Changes to major capital financing arrangements can indicate completion, write-off, or restructuring of significant capital projects that affect the council's debt position","iso":"2026-09-14","paper_title":"Enc. 4 for Audit Committee Annual Report 25/26","paper_url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk/documents/s184889/184889.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Committee identified a breach of financial regulation 18.6 requiring investigation","lead":"Breaches of financial regulation indicate control weaknesses that could affect the council's financial management and compliance","iso":"2026-09-14","paper_title":"Enc. 4 for Audit Committee Annual Report 25/26","paper_url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk/documents/s184889/184889.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"East Midlands Shared Services Joint Committee","paper_title":"EMSS Finance Report JC Sep 26 (Final)","paper_url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk/documents/s185210/185210.pdf","headline":"EMSS operational reserves fully depleted; partnership now has no financial buffer against overspends","detail":"EMSS had historically maintained reserves for redundancy costs, invest-to-save initiatives, and exceptional operational demand. The Joint Committee approved removal of these reserves in June 2022 when they fell below best practice. The balance has now been fully depleted. Going forward, Nottingham City Council and Leicestershire County Council must fund any operational overspend on a 50:50 basis with no contingency available.","category":"reserves","matters":"Nottingham City's financial resilience is affected by its reliance on EMSS; the elimination of reserves means unexpected operational costs must now be funded immediately from the council's own budget rather than from partnership contingencies","figures":"Reserve balance now \u00a30; NCC contribution to EMSS \u00a33.226 million (55.45% of \u00a35.818 million partnership funding)","quote":"The balance of reserve has now been fully depleted and partners will be expected to fund any operational overspend arising in year on a 50:50 basis.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"City Council","paper_title":"Enc. 4 for Audit Committee Annual Report 25/26","paper_url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk/documents/s184889/184889.pdf","headline":"Nottingham Castle loan was removed from the 2026/27 Capital Strategy","detail":"The committee recommended updating the Capital Strategy to reflect removal of the Nottingham Castle loan from Table 7. This was accepted and approved by full Council in March 2026. The specific reason for removal or financial implications are not detailed in this document.","category":"capital","matters":"Changes to major capital financing arrangements can indicate completion, write-off, or restructuring of significant capital projects that affect the council's debt position","figures":"Nottingham Castle loan removal","quote":"Recommended to Council that the Capital Strategy is approved subject to Table 7 being updated in regards to the removal of the Nottingham Castle loan.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"City Council","paper_title":"Enc. 4 for Audit Committee Annual Report 25/26","paper_url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk/documents/s184889/184889.pdf","headline":"Committee identified a breach of financial regulation 18.6 requiring investigation","detail":"In the March 2026 Internal Audit Progress Report, the committee requested additional details on the breach of financial regulation 18.6. Additional details were circulated to committee members in April 2026, though not included in this document.","category":"audit","matters":"Breaches of financial regulation indicate control weaknesses that could affect the council's financial management and compliance","figures":"breach of financial regulation 18.6","quote":"Request additional details on the breach of financial regulation 18.6.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"City Council","paper_title":"2. Motion in the name of Cllr Kirsty L Jones - Cease the use of Bailiffs for Families with Children and to Review Practices of Council Tax Debt Collection in Nottingham","paper_url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk/documents/s184890/184890.pdf","headline":"Bailiff enforcement extracts high costs that consume collection proceeds, leaving only 27p per \u00a31 for the council.","detail":"Bailiff fees and administrative costs are substantial. A 2018 Freedom of Information request found that councils recover only 27p net from every \u00a31 referred to bailiffs, meaning 73% of the enforcement cost is absorbed by bailiff fees and administration rather than reaching the council.","category":"income","matters":"The council is using a revenue collection method where three-quarters of the enforcement cost evaporates; this represents financial inefficiency in managing a critical revenue stream and suggests alternative collection methods might be more cost-effective.","figures":"27p per \u00a31 referred","quote":"A 2018 Freedom of Information request showed that for every \u00a31 referred to bailiffs for collection, councils only received 27p.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"City Council","paper_title":"2. Motion in the name of Cllr Kirsty L Jones - Cease the use of Bailiffs for Families with Children and to Review Practices of Council Tax Debt Collection in Nottingham","paper_url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk/documents/s184890/184890.pdf","headline":"Council tax bailiff referrals surged 34% in one year while collection recovery remains weak.","detail":"Bailiff referrals rose from 17,361 cases in 2024/25 to 23,294 in 2025/26. Nationally, bailiffs recovered only \u00a3255.8m of \u00a3755.3m referred in 2025 (34% recovery). Nottingham's historical experience is worse: in 2014/15, only \u00a32.2m was recovered from \u00a39.2m referred (24% recovery).","category":"income","matters":"Council tax is a primary revenue stream; rising enforcement referrals despite low recovery rates suggests collection is deteriorating and the council is relying on an ineffective mechanism to cover a growing collection gap.","figures":"2024/25: 17,361 cases; 2025/26: 23,294 cases (34% increase); nationally 2025: \u00a3255.8m recovered of \u00a3755.3m referred; Nottingham 2014/15: \u00a32.2m recovered of \u00a39.2m referred","quote":"In 2024/25, Nottingham City Council referred 17,361 cases of council tax arrears for enforcement action. In 2025/26, this number had risen to 23,294.","material":true}],"editorial":{"headline":"Nottingham City audit opinion disclaimed as joint venture reserves depleted","standfirst":"Depleted reserves in a 50:50 partnership and governance weaknesses have left the council without financial buffer or clean audit bill.","body":"Nottingham City Council faces a compounding set of financial and governance pressures that have left it without the reserves to absorb further shocks.\n\nThe council is a 50:50 partner in East Midlands Shared Services. As of 31 March 2026, EMSS reserves were fully depleted. The council's papers presented to June's joint committee meeting state: \"The balance of reserve has now been fully depleted and partners will be expected to fund any operational overspend arising in year on a 50:50 basis.\" This leaves Nottingham City liable for half of any cost overrun.\n\nA month later, Grant Thornton, the council's external auditor, issued a disclaimed audit opinion on the 2025/26 accounts\u2014a rare step indicating it cannot form a view on the reliability of the financial statements. The council's papers presented to July's corporate scrutiny committee describe the work underway: \"Council works closely with the External Auditor to identify the key information and evidential requirements that the auditor will need to provide a positive non-qualified audit opinion from a standard base disclaimed opinion.\"\n\nBut internal audit tells a deeper story. As of 31 July, the Head of Internal Audit's annual opinion for 2025/26 provides only Moderate Assurance. The audit committee papers state: \"Governance and control improvements are not yet consistently embedded across all areas.\" Multiple service areas remain problematic. The papers identify Adult Social Care performance and statutory compliance as an ongoing governance issue. Children's Services and Housing Services require continued improvement. Workforce capacity and resilience are constrained.\n\nCompounding these issues is a pay award pressure. The council budgeted 3.5%, but the employers' current offer stands at 3.2%. The EMSS papers note: \"Early indications suggest this offer will be rejected, with negotiations ongoing. Consequently, there remains a risk that the final award may be higher than the provision made in the budget for 2026/27.\"\n\nThe combination\u2014zero reserves, audit failure, governance weaknesses not yet embedded\u2014leaves little room for manoeuvre if budget pressures intensify.","pull_quote":"Governance and control improvements are not yet consistently embedded across all areas","watch_next":"Grant Thornton is targeting a non-qualified audit opinion by March 2028, when the council's remediation efforts must demonstrate concrete results.","based_on_iso":"2026-07-31"},"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-14"},"name":"Nottingham City UA","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-17","since_iso":"2026-08-18","distress":false,"headline":"Nottingham Castle loan was removed from the 2026/27 Capital Strategy","lead":"Changes to major capital financing arrangements can indicate completion, write-off, or restructuring of significant capital projects that affect the council's debt position","also":["4 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Grant Thornton is targeting a non-qualified audit opinion by March 2028, when the council's remediation efforts must demonstrate concrete results.","n_findings":5,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Enc. 4 for Audit Committee Annual Report 25/26","top_paper_url":"https://committee.nottinghamcity.gov.uk/documents/s184889/184889.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":110,"news_drafts_total":3}}